  'f.prep1.t': 'Step 1 · Turn the message into bytes',
  'f.prep1.s': 'A computer understands <b>numbers</b>, not letters. AES splits the message into blocks of <b>16 bytes</b>. 1 byte = 8 bits = 2 hexadecimal (hex) digits, for example {ex}. Below are our 16 bytes.',
  'f.prep2.t': 'Step 2 · The 4×4 table (state)',
  'f.prep2.s': 'AES places the bytes in a <b>4×4 table</b> and calls it the <b>state</b>. The order matters: first <b>top to bottom</b>, and when a column is full — on to the next one. From now on all the encryption happens on this table.',
  'f.prep3.t': 'Step 3 · The key',
  'f.prep3.s': 'The key is also 16 bytes and is placed in a 4×4 table the same way. The key is your <b>password</b>: without it nobody can open the message. Later we will derive <b>11 round keys</b> from this key — one for each stage.',
  'f.prep4.t': 'Step 4 · The plan',
  'f.prep4.s': 'Now the work begins. First <b>round 0</b> (only the key is added). Then <b>9 full rounds</b>: SubBytes → ShiftRows → MixColumns → AddRoundKey. The last one, <b>round 10</b>, has no MixColumns. We will go through every operation slowly, one step at a time.',
  'f.kg.t': 'Key generation · round {r} key',
  'f.kg1.s': 'Every round needs <b>its own key</b>. We supplied only one 16-byte key, so AES creates 10 more from it through <b>key expansion</b>. A key consists of 4 <b>words</b> (4 bytes each — one column of the table). Each new round key is built from the previous one: the <b>1st word</b> is a bit more complex, the other 3 are plain XOR. In total 11 keys = 44 words.',
  'f.kgN.s': 'After round {p} we need a new key. We build it from the <b>round {p} key</b>: the 1st word with a complex formula, the other 3 words with plain XOR. Watch the key fill up on the right.',
  'f.kgw.t': 'Key generation · word {n}',
  'f.kgw0.s': 'The first word is <b>trickier</b>: take the last word of the previous key → <b>rotate</b> its bytes (RotWord) → pass each through the <b>S-box</b> (SubWord) → mix in <b>Rcon</b> (a different constant every round) → and XOR with the 1st word of the round {p} key.',
  'f.kgwN.s': 'The remaining words are simple: <b>the previous word</b> ⊕ <b>the word in the same place in the earlier key</b>. This chain "stretches" the whole key.',
  'f.ark0.t': 'AddRoundKey · Mixing in the key',
  'f.ark0.s': 'First job: XOR every byte of the table with the <b>matching byte of the key</b>. XOR is the "1 if different, 0 if same" rule. Here the key mixes into the data <b>for the first time</b>.',
  'f.ark.t': 'AddRoundKey · with the round {r} key',
  'f.ark.s': 'Now we XOR every byte of the table with the round {r} key we just made (on the right). The key takes part only here — the secrecy of the cipher comes from exactly this.',
  'f.xor.t': 'XOR · byte {n} of 16',
  'f.xor.s': 'We compare byte {n} of the state {a} and the key byte {k} bit by bit. If the bits differ — <b>1</b>, if they are the same — <b>0</b>.',
  'f.sub.t': 'SubBytes · The secret substitution table',
  'f.sub1.s': 'Each byte is <b>replaced with another byte</b> through a 16×16 table called the <b>S-box</b>. A byte has 2 hex digits: the first is the <b>row</b>, the second is the <b>column</b>. The number at that spot in the table is the new byte. This is the only <b>"non-linear"</b> step in AES: without it the cipher could be solved with simple equations. The S-box is not random — it comes from a special math formula (the inverse number in GF(2⁸)).',
  'f.subN.s': 'S-box again: each byte is looked up in the table and replaced with another. Same as round 1 — row = first digit, column = second digit.',
  'f.look.t': 'SubBytes · byte {n} · table lookup',
  'f.look.s': 'Byte {b} → row {hi}, column {lo} → the S-box number is {out}. How this number ended up in the table we will see in the next step.',
  'f.calc.t': 'SubBytes · byte {n} · the S-box formula',
  'f.calc.s': 'The S-box table is not "written by hand" — it is computed in 2 actions: <b>1)</b> find the byte\'s <b>inverse</b> in GF(2⁸), <b>2)</b> scramble the bits with a special <b>affine formula</b>. The result {out} equals the table.',
  'f.sh.t': 'ShiftRows · Shifting the rows',
  'f.sh.s': 'We shift the rows of the table <b>left</b>: row 0 — not at all, row 1 — by 1, row 2 — by 2, row 3 — by 3. A byte that falls off the edge <b>wraps around to the end</b> of the row. Why? This step <b>spreads</b> bytes across different columns, and MixColumns then mixes within a column — together they make every byte affect every other byte.',
  'f.sh0.t': 'ShiftRows · row 0', 'f.sh0.s': 'Row 0 <b>does not shift</b> — the bytes stay in place. We start shifting from the next rows.',
  'f.shs.t': 'ShiftRows · row {row} · shift {st} of {row}',
  'f.shs.s': 'We shift row {row} <b>one place left</b>. Every byte moves one cell left, and the leftmost {b} <b>wraps around to the end</b> of the row.',
  'f.shs.s2': 'We shift row {row} <b>one place left</b> ({row} times in total, this is number {st}). Every byte moves one cell left, and the leftmost {b} <b>wraps around to the end</b> of the row.',
  'f.ml0.t': 'Primer · A byte is really a polynomial',
  'f.ml0.s': 'To understand MixColumns we need one idea: <b>every byte is a polynomial</b>. 8 bits = 8 coefficients. If a bit is 1, the power of {x} at that position is present, if 0 it is absent. For example, let us write the byte {hex57} as a polynomial.',
  'f.ml1.t': 'Primer · Adding polynomials = XOR',
  'f.ml1.s': 'When you add polynomials, equal powers <b>cancel out</b> (1+1=0). So addition is exactly <b>XOR</b>. To add two bytes we XOR them bit by bit. There is no carry at all!',
  'f.ml2.t': 'Primer · Multiplying by x = shift left',
  'f.ml2.s': 'If we multiply by {x}, every power goes up by 1: {x2} → {x3}. In terms of bits this is <b>shifting all bits one place left</b>. Here is {hex57} multiplied by {x}.',
  'f.ml3.t': 'Primer · What if it does not fit?',
  'f.ml3.s': 'A byte is only 8 bits. If there was an {x7} before multiplying, it becomes {x8} — it <b>does not fit</b>. The AES rule: {rule}. So we drop the bit that fell out and <b>XOR</b> the result with {c1b} (= {h1b}).',
  'f.ml4.t': 'Primer · ×1, ×2, ×3',
  'f.ml4.s': 'MixColumns uses only three coefficients: <b>01</b> (unchanged), <b>02</b> (= {x}, so we shift), <b>03</b> (= {x1}, so we shift <b>and</b> add the number itself/XOR). That is all!',
  'f.ml5.t': 'Primer · The matrix',
  'f.ml5.s': 'Each column is "multiplied" by the matrix below. The rule is simple: each new byte = the <b>sum of coefficient products</b> of the column\'s 4 bytes (sum = XOR). Now we apply this rule step by step for 4 columns, 4 new bytes in each.',
  'f.mr.t': 'MixColumns · Reminder',
  'f.mr.s': 'Each column is multiplied by the matrix: new byte = {f} (cyclically). Multiplication: <b>×1</b> unchanged, <b>×2</b> shift left (if it does not fit, XOR with {h1b}), <b>×3</b> = ×2 ⊕ the number itself.',
  'f.mc.t': 'MixColumns · column {c}',
  'f.mc.s': 'Now we take column {c}: {vals}. From it we make <b>4 new bytes</b>. Each one needs 4 products and their XOR sum.',
  'f.mt.t': 'b{rr} (column {c}) · term {k}',
  'f.mt.s1': 'Term {k}: multiply {a} by <b>01</b> — nothing changes.',
  'f.mt.s2': 'Term {k}: multiply {a} by <b>02</b>, i.e. by {x} — shift the bits left.',
  'f.mt.s3': 'Term {k}: multiply {a} by <b>03</b>, i.e. by {x1} — shift and also add the number itself.',
  'f.ms.t': 'b{rr} (column {c}) · Sum',
  'f.ms.s': 'We add the 4 terms with <b>XOR</b> (adding polynomials = XOR). The result {res} is the new byte in the table.',
  'f.res.t': 'Done! The encrypted text',
